A||||5||Homicidal||Gear Shift or Tranny Problems?  ||||1||
Z||000000||Homicidal||06-07-2000||01:10 AM||||I got an odd problem, nothing cripling but sure is annoying.<P>Say I am in a gear and I come to a stoplight and I release the clutch pedal and just sit there.  When the light turns green to go, and I just press the clutch pedal down and shift into first gear, I hear a BAD grinding noise, like I didn't press the clutch pedal down!<P>BUT, if I press the pedal down and shift into 4th, 3rd, or 2nd BEFORE shifting into first, it's fine.  What is causing this?  I can't sit there and then go, I gotta shift into 3rd first then 1st.  More time consuming than you think!<P>What I SUSPECT is that my tranny is going bad so when I press the clutch down, the gears are still spinning when I put it into first.  But putting it into another gear stops the gears.<P>Correct?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||06-07-2000||02:22 AM||||Second first gets to be habit pretty quickly, not as slow as seems, most shifting into gear can be anticipated.<BR>Fourth reverse actually saves time by not having to wait for the reverse gear to slow down.<BR>Push the lever gently towards first gear, the syncro's can be felt as the stoppers rub against them, steady gentle pressure should stop the gear then a firm push meshes the gears. SLOW like fifty weight, now the light is red again.<P>Long term damage? Haven't noticed any on mine in the last 30,000.<BR>But where does the metal go?  The syncro rings are bronze, does the dust plate the gears and make them smoother?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homicidal||06-07-2000||02:28 AM||||But the main question is, how do I fix this?  Where is the problem located, tranny, clutch (put a new clutch in, problem still exists), or gearshift?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||06-07-2000||02:42 AM||||The syncro rides a shaft inside the tranny.<BR>No easy fix, the tranny has to be pulled.<P>Losing the first gear syncro is like having back pain, It is so prevalent it has to be considered normal after a certain age.<P>Change the tranny fluid, if lots of sparkles show in the drainpan then maybe change it again in a week just because.<P>Ever doubleclutched from second to first at speed?  Wheee,it is a sense of accomplishment that just cannot be found by pushing hard through a good syncro.<P>Old transmissions did not have syncro's at all in any gear! Avoid grinding and no further damage will happen.<P>Many dollars and a fair amount of time will fix it completely.  Until it wears out again.||||reg||12
Z||000004||Homicidal||06-07-2000||08:32 PM||||I appreciate the help, but I can't fully understand what you mean!<P>So.....it IS a tranny problem, right?<P>What is a "doubleclutch"?<P>And how do I remedy this problem, new transmission?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phaeton||06-07-2000||10:53 PM||||The sychronizer ring rides a shaft beside first gear.  Engaging the gear slides the gear and synchro down the shaft.  The pointy ends on the synchro ring run into a stopper and the gear and the synchro quit spinning and the gear can now mesh with the mainshaft gear without grinding.<BR>When the pointy ends of the synchro wear down the gear does not stop as quickly and is still spinning enough to grind when shifted fast.<BR>Slow shifts give the stopper more time to slow down the gear, reducing or eliminating the mismatch.<BR>First gear goes out because it is the gear that has the highest relative spin that must be stopped.<P>The repair consists of removing the transmission, taking it apart and putting a new synchronizer in.  The part is relatively inexpensive compared to the 8 to 12 hours of labor involved.  With shop time running from 50 to 75 dollars an hour it gets expensive very rapidly.<BR>Due to the extraordinary amount of labor many drivers do not repair this unless the tranny is coming out for some other problem also.<BR>This will not break the transmission unless it is wholeheartedly abused to the point of chipping the ends off the gears, a very noisy process.<P>This is more of a nuisance than a transmission threatening problem.<BR>Changing the fluid will get the bronze dust from the synchro out of the transmission, giving peace of mind, but not relief from the frustration of sticking the Fiero SLOWLY into first gear.<P>Any obtuseness on my part is because I can't shape things with my hands or wave my arms like spinning gears.  OK I can, but nobody notices.||||reg||12
